<root BTCPP_format="4" main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
    <!--
    Phase 1 of the real-vision pipeline: "identify + normalize".

    Picks the die blind (its true yaw is unknown -- the real vision service
    reports face number and position reliably, but only a placeholder
    orientation), tips it 90deg about world_Y to expose a second face,
    reads that face's pip count, resolves the true yaw from the
    (current_face, second_face) pair, and finally puts the die back on the
    table in a CANONICAL orientation (yaw = 0).

    Why it ends with a canonical placement rather than just reporting the
    resolved yaw: the first pick necessarily happens BEFORE the yaw is
    known, and GetGraspOrientation's yaw table assumes a canonical die.
    Verified numerically over all (face, yaw, target) combinations: a blind
    pick closes on the pair the table assumes only half the time (60/120
    mismatched) and puts a finger directly on the target face in 48/120 --
    the "a finger is on the face we need" failure seen on the real robot.
    Re-checked after a canonical placement: 0/30. So phase 2 (the existing
    ReorientCycle, unchanged) becomes correct by construction.

    Everything here is analytic: no face{N}_tf lookups (simulator-only,
    absent on the real robot) and no trust in any reported orientation.
    -->
    <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
        <Sequence>
            <Fallback>
                <DetachObject service_name="/detach_object" object_id="dice"/>
                <AlwaysSuccess/>
            </Fallback>
            <Fallback>
                <GripperCommand action_name="/gripper_action_controller/gripper_cmd" position="0.043" max_effort="1.0"/>
                <AlwaysSuccess/>
            </Fallback>
            <MoveToJoint action_name="/move_to_joint" joint_target="1.5708;-1.97;2.13;-1.83;-1.50;0.0"/>

            <!-- Blind pick: only the face number is trusted here. The
                 degenerate target_face==current_face call just asks for the
                 standard tilted grasp; which pair it ends up pinching is
                 unknown at this point, and deliberately not relied upon. -->
            <DiceIdentification service_name="/dice_identification" face_number="{face1}" pose="{dice_pose1}"/>
            <!-- The tip axis is declared here because the GRASP depends on
                 it too: the closing axis must be parallel to the tip axis,
                 otherwise the tip drives it vertical and the face it
                 exposes is one the fingers are holding. Measured over all 6
                 faces x 4 yaw hypotheses: parallel -> 0/24 occluded,
                 perpendicular -> 24/24. Both stay fully informative. -->
            <GetGraspOrientation current_face="{face1}" target_face="{face1}" pick_orientation="{pick_orientation}" grasp_yaw_deg="{grasp_yaw}"/>
            <RetryUntilSuccessful num_attempts="2">
                <MoveToPose action_name="/move_to_pose" cartesian_motion="false" frame_id="dice_tf" position="0.0;0.0;0.0" orientation="{pick_orientation}"/>
            </RetryUntilSuccessful>
            <Fallback>
                <GripperCommand action_name="/gripper_action_controller/gripper_cmd" position="0.0" max_effort="1.0"/>
                <AlwaysSuccess/>
            </Fallback>
            <AttachObject service_name="/attach_object" object_id="dice" target_frame_id="tool0"/>

            <!-- Lift AND centre over the play area in one straight-line
                 move (safe to combine: translation only, no reorientation).
                 The target is the centre of the camera frame's floor plate
                 -- on the real cell the camera is mounted above that
                 structure and looks down at it, so it IS the field the die
                 is thrown onto and read in. (0.365, 0.265) is the plate's
                 centre in camera_frame_floor: the plate is a 0.63 x 0.43
                 box whose visual origin sits at (frame_x/2, frame_y/2) with
                 frame_x=0.73, frame_y=0.53, beam=0.05 in
                 ur5e_cell.urdf.xacro. Expressing it in that frame rather
                 than as tuned base_link numbers keeps it correct if the
                 cell or the robot's mounting angle changes.
                 NOTE for simulation: in configuration_cell_1.yaml the
                 camera (child of base_link, at 0.6/0/0.8) and this plate
                 (child of table_top) are placed independently and do NOT
                 line up -- the camera looks at base_link (0.600, 0.000)
                 while the plate centre is at (-0.095, 0.685). That is a
                 cell-config inconsistency, harmless in sim (identification
                 is a cheat service that reads TF, not the camera) but worth
                 fixing before trusting the sim's camera view. -->
            <ComputeXYCorrection current_pose="{dice_pose1}" target_frame="camera_frame_floor" target_x="0.365" target_y="0.265" z_offset="0.10" xy_correction="{centre_lift}"/>
            <RetryUntilSuccessful num_attempts="2">
                <MoveToPose action_name="/move_to_pose" relative_motion="true" cartesian_motion="true" frame_id="base_link" position="{centre_lift}" orientation="0.0;0.0;0.0;1.0"/>
            </RetryUntilSuccessful>

            <!-- The informative tip: 90deg about a fixed WORLD axis. The
                 axis is named ONCE here and drives both the quaternion
                 actually commanded (GetTipRotation) and the table used to
                 resolve it (ResolveDieOrientation), so the two can't drift
                 apart into a wrong-but-plausible yaw. All four candidate
                 axes are equally valid (each injective, verified
                 numerically); which one the wrist can reach depends on the
                 cell, home pose and die position -- world_Y+ was validated
                 earlier but became NO_IK_SOLUTION after the home pose moved
                 to shoulder_pan=90deg and the spawn position changed, so
                 world_X+ is used here. cartesian_motion=false: a 90deg
                 reorientation doesn't survive straight-line interpolation.

                 frame_id="world", NOT "base_link": this cell's base_link is
                 rotated -90deg about Z relative to world (robot.rpy in
                 configuration_cell_N.yaml), measured directly via TF. The
                 older comments in this project claiming the two share an
                 orientation are wrong. It matters here because the tables
                 are derived in the frame where the face normals and "up"
                 live (world, which is what /dice_identification reports
                 in): commanding the same quaternion in base_link executed a
                 world_Y- tip instead of world_X+, so the resolver read the
                 wrong table and produced a yaw ~100deg off. Confirmed by
                 cross-check: the face the robot actually read matches the
                 world_Y- table exactly. -->
            <!-- The tip axis is chosen from the closing axis the grasp
                 actually produced (measured via TF), not fixed in advance:
                 the tip must be PARALLEL to the closing axis, otherwise it
                 drives that axis vertical and the face it exposes is one
                 the fingers are holding. Measured over all 6 faces x 4 yaw
                 hypotheses: parallel -> 0/24 occluded, perpendicular ->
                 24/24, both equally informative. Choosing the axis to match
                 the grasp (rather than forcing the grasp to match a chosen
                 axis) keeps the existing, kinematically-proven pick
                 untouched. -->
            <SelectTipAxis world_frame="world" gripper_frame="tool0" prefer_negative="false" tip_axis="{tip_axis}" alignment="{tip_alignment}"/>
            <GetTipRotation tip_axis="{tip_axis}" orientation="{tip_quat}" inverse_orientation="{tip_quat_inv}"/>
            <RetryUntilSuccessful num_attempts="2">
                <MoveToPose action_name="/move_to_pose" relative_motion="true" cartesian_motion="false" frame_id="world" position="0.0;0.0;0.0" orientation="{tip_quat}"/>
            </RetryUntilSuccessful>
            <!-- Evidence that the exposed face is actually readable: with
                 the closing axis parallel to the tip axis it should stay
                 horizontal (|z| ~ 0), meaning the fingers hold two side
                 faces and not the one now facing the camera. Wrapped so it
                 only measures and logs, never blocks. -->
            <Fallback>
                <CheckClosingAxisVertical base_frame="base_link" gripper_frame="tool0" threshold="0.7" is_vertical="{pinch_vertical}"/>
                <AlwaysSuccess/>
            </Fallback>
            <DiceIdentification service_name="/dice_identification" face_number="{face2}" pose="{dice_pose2}"/>
            <ResolveDieOrientation current_face="{face1}" second_face="{face2}" tip_axis="{tip_axis}" resolved_theta_deg="{theta}"/>

            <!-- Undo the tip before placing. Rotating straight out of the
                 tipped pose to the canonical placement was observed to hit
                 NO_IK_SOLUTION; undoing first is reachable by construction
                 (it returns the wrist to the pose it was in a moment ago)
                 and leaves the placement rotation starting from the known-
                 good pick configuration. Same lesson as elsewhere in this
                 project: two smaller moves beat one large one. -->
            <RetryUntilSuccessful num_attempts="2">
                <MoveToPose action_name="/move_to_pose" relative_motion="true" cartesian_motion="false" frame_id="world" position="0.0;0.0;0.0" orientation="{tip_quat_inv}"/>
            </RetryUntilSuccessful>

            <!-- Rotate to a canonical (yaw = 0) placement, chosen so the
                 closing axis stays horizontal at release. Verified: such a
                 placement exists for every (face, yaw), always with the
                 closing axis exactly horizontal; rotations are 90 or 120deg.
                 tip_orientation is identity here: the tip has just been
                 undone, so no net rotation has been applied since the pick. -->
            <ComputeCanonicalPlacement current_face="{face1}" resolved_theta_deg="{theta}" pick_orientation="{pick_orientation}" tip_orientation="0.0;0.0;0.0;1.0" orientation="{canonical_rot}" placed_face="{placed_face}"/>
            <RetryUntilSuccessful num_attempts="2">
                <MoveToPose action_name="/move_to_pose" relative_motion="true" cartesian_motion="false" frame_id="world" position="0.0;0.0;0.0" orientation="{canonical_rot}"/>
            </RetryUntilSuccessful>

            <!-- Place back down centred under the camera. Re-reads the pose
                 first because the canonical rotation shifts the die a few
                 cm (the rotation pivot isn't the die's centre), then
                 corrects XY back to the plate centre and Z down to the
                 die's resting height on the PLATE -- the camera frame's
                 floor plate is the surface the die actually sits on, not
                 the table: measured base_link z -0.050 for the plate, +5mm
                 thickness, +15mm half a die = -0.030 -- in one
                 move -- same pattern ReorientCycle already uses. -->
            <DiceIdentification service_name="/dice_identification" face_number="{face_mid}" pose="{dice_pose_mid}"/>
            <ComputeXYCorrection current_pose="{dice_pose_mid}" target_frame="camera_frame_floor" target_x="0.365" target_y="0.265" target_z="-0.03" xy_correction="{centre_place}"/>
            <RetryUntilSuccessful num_attempts="2">
                <MoveToPose action_name="/move_to_pose" relative_motion="true" cartesian_motion="true" frame_id="base_link" position="{centre_place}" orientation="0.0;0.0;0.0;1.0"/>
            </RetryUntilSuccessful>
            <Fallback>
                <GripperCommand action_name="/gripper_action_controller/gripper_cmd" position="0.045" max_effort="1.0"/>
                <AlwaysSuccess/>
            </Fallback>
            <Fallback>
                <DetachObject service_name="/detach_object" object_id="dice"/>
                <AlwaysSuccess/>
            </Fallback>

            <!-- Verification read: the face now up must equal placed_face.
                 Logged by bt_executor_node so a run can be checked at a
                 glance. -->
            <DiceIdentification service_name="/dice_identification" face_number="{face3}" pose="{dice_pose3}"/>
            <MoveToJoint action_name="/move_to_joint" joint_target="1.5708;-1.97;2.13;-1.83;-1.50;0.0"/>
        </Sequence>
    </BehaviorTree>
</root>
